1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,908
There's a whole country hidden inside Rome.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:07,663
This is Vatican City, known as the smallest country in the world.

3
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:10,123
Its capital is Vatican City.

4
00:00:10,870 --> 00:00:12,601
About eight hundred eighty people live here.

5
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:15,394
It covers less than half a square kilometer.

6
00:00:16,140 --> 00:00:18,197
They speak Italian and Latin.

7
00:00:18,247 --> 00:00:20,522
In Italian, hello is buongiorno.

8
00:00:20,572 --> 00:00:22,048
In Latin, it's salve.

9
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:23,996
And they pay with the euro.

10
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:27,311
Some fun facts.

11
00:00:27,361 --> 00:00:30,965
And stay until the end, for a mystery nobody ever solved.

12
00:00:32,483 --> 00:00:34,720
It has the shortest railway in the world.

13
00:00:34,770 --> 00:00:37,563
The main tracks are only three hundred meters long.

14
00:00:38,113 --> 00:00:41,686
There's a cash machine where you can choose Latin as your language.

15
00:00:42,236 --> 00:00:46,800
The entire country is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, the only one like it.

16
00:00:47,667 --> 00:00:49,524
Now, a few surprising places.

17
00:00:51,017 --> 00:00:55,515
The famous spiral staircase everyone photographs dates from nineteen thirty two.

18
00:00:55,565 --> 00:00:59,439
The original, from the early fifteen hundreds, is closed to visitors.

19
00:00:59,989 --> 00:01:03,714
In the Pinecone Courtyard stands a giant bronze pinecone,

20
00:01:03,764 --> 00:01:05,569
over two thousand years old,

21
00:01:05,619 --> 00:01:07,623
guarded by two bronze peacocks.

22
00:01:08,173 --> 00:01:10,655
The Vatican Gardens are one of its hidden gems.

23
00:01:10,705 --> 00:01:12,488
Only guided tour groups can go in.

24
00:01:13,038 --> 00:01:14,196
In the Raphael Rooms,

25
00:01:14,246 --> 00:01:17,129
a Swiss Guard is painted carrying the pope himself,

26
00:01:17,179 --> 00:01:19,660
in a fresco from the early fifteen hundreds.

27
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,187
So, what does ten dollars get you here?

28
00:01:24,717 --> 00:01:27,521
An espresso at the counter costs about one dollar fifty.

29
00:01:28,071 --> 00:01:30,201
A bus ticket costs about one dollar seventy.

30
00:01:30,751 --> 00:01:33,226
A slice of pizza costs about four dollars.

31
00:01:33,776 --> 00:01:36,448
Two scoops of gelato cost about four dollars.

32
00:01:36,998 --> 00:01:40,449
But a Vatican Museums ticket costs about thirty dollars,

33
00:01:40,499 --> 00:01:42,513
more than most museums back home.

34
00:01:43,063 --> 00:01:44,288
So with ten dollars,

35
00:01:44,338 --> 00:01:45,563
you get an espresso,

36
00:01:45,613 --> 00:01:46,647
a slice of pizza,

37
00:01:46,697 --> 00:01:47,476
and a gelato,

38
00:01:47,526 --> 00:01:49,007
but not a museum ticket.

39
00:01:49,867 --> 00:01:50,770
Now, the don't list.

40
00:01:52,283 --> 00:01:54,283
Don't wear shorts or tank tops.

41
00:01:54,333 --> 00:01:57,060
Only visitors dressed respectfully get in.

42
00:01:57,610 --> 00:02:00,606
Don't flash your camera anywhere in the museums.

43
00:02:00,656 --> 00:02:02,890
Flash photos are strictly forbidden.

44
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:05,568
Don't take photos in the Sistine Chapel.

45
00:02:05,618 --> 00:02:08,670
You can photograph almost everywhere else, but not there.

46
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:11,804
Don't sit or eat on monuments in Rome.

47
00:02:11,854 --> 00:02:14,507
It can bring a fine, or even an arrest.

48
00:02:15,057 --> 00:02:18,381
And don't walk around Rome shirtless or in swimwear.

49
00:02:18,431 --> 00:02:19,419
It's an offence.

50
00:02:20,267 --> 00:02:21,370
Let's talk about culture.

51
00:02:22,917 --> 00:02:24,422
There's no national dish,

52
00:02:24,472 --> 00:02:26,102
but just outside the walls,

53
00:02:26,152 --> 00:02:27,408
Romans eat carbonara,

54
00:02:27,458 --> 00:02:29,337
fried rice balls called suppli,

55
00:02:29,387 --> 00:02:30,768
and pizza by the slice.

56
00:02:31,318 --> 00:02:34,059
The Swiss Guard uniform looks like the Renaissance,

57
00:02:34,109 --> 00:02:36,302
but it was approved in nineteen fourteen.

58
00:02:36,352 --> 00:02:38,382
And no, Michelangelo didn't design it.

59
00:02:38,932 --> 00:02:41,701
And the sound of the Vatican is the human voice.

60
00:02:41,751 --> 00:02:46,284
The pope's own choir, the Sistine Chapel Choir, sings without any instruments.

61
00:02:47,133 --> 00:02:48,307
Time for a little history.

62
00:02:49,850 --> 00:02:50,953
In eighteen seventy,

63
00:02:51,003 --> 00:02:52,682
Italy took Rome from the pope,

64
00:02:52,732 --> 00:02:55,565
who then called himself a prisoner of the Vatican.

65
00:02:56,115 --> 00:03:01,115
In nineteen twenty nine, the Lateran Treaty made Vatican City an independent state.

66
00:03:01,665 --> 00:03:03,039
In twenty twenty five,

67
00:03:03,089 --> 00:03:05,693
the cardinals elected Leo the fourteenth,

68
00:03:05,743 --> 00:03:08,152
the first pope from the United States.

69
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,141
And now, the chapter you were all waiting for: legends and mysteries.

70
00:03:16,617 --> 00:03:17,945
In the twelfth century,

71
00:03:17,995 --> 00:03:23,040
an Irish archbishop named Saint Malachy supposedly had a vision of every future pope.

72
00:03:23,090 --> 00:03:28,494
His list of one hundred twelve short Latin phrases ends with a pope called Peter the Roman.

73
00:03:28,544 --> 00:03:31,491
But it was first published in fifteen ninety five,

74
00:03:31,541 --> 00:03:34,068
more than four hundred years after he died.

75
00:03:34,118 --> 00:03:36,286
Most historians think it's a forgery,

76
00:03:36,336 --> 00:03:39,343
yet it comes back every time a new pope is elected.

77
00:03:39,893 --> 00:03:42,244
The legend says a woman once became pope,

78
00:03:42,294 --> 00:03:44,176
around the year eight fifty five,

79
00:03:44,226 --> 00:03:45,289
disguised as a man.

80
00:03:45,339 --> 00:03:48,979
The story only appears in chronicles in the thirteenth century,

81
00:03:49,029 --> 00:03:50,794
about four hundred years later.

82
00:03:50,844 --> 00:03:53,781
Even her name changes from one version to the next.

83
00:03:53,831 --> 00:03:57,237
Historians find no place for her in the historical sources,

84
00:03:57,287 --> 00:03:59,169
and most agree she never existed.

85
00:03:59,219 --> 00:04:01,395
But the tale has lasted for centuries.

86
00:04:01,945 --> 00:04:03,187
On June twenty second,

87
00:04:03,237 --> 00:04:04,480
nineteen eighty three,

88
00:04:04,530 --> 00:04:10,590
fifteen year old Emanuela Orlandi left her family's home inside Vatican City for a music lesson in Rome.

89
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:11,765
She never came home.

90
00:04:11,815 --> 00:04:13,763
Her father worked for the Vatican.

91
00:04:13,813 --> 00:04:16,760
Both Italy and the Vatican reopened investigations,

92
00:04:16,810 --> 00:04:19,051
and one Vatican probe was later closed.

93
00:04:19,101 --> 00:04:23,223
After more than forty years, nobody has explained what happened to her.

94
00:04:24,767 --> 00:04:25,964
That was Vatican City.

95
00:04:26,014 --> 00:04:27,947
Which country should we visit next?

96
00:04:27,997 --> 00:04:29,308
Tell me in the comments.
